<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-divergence-theorem" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>The Divergence Theorem</title>

<introduction>
<p>
In <xref ref="section-greens-theorem"/> we saw that the divergence of a
two-dimensional field measures its flux density, and Green's theorem
(in its normal form) converted a flux integral around a closed curve
into a double integral of the divergence. In this section we extend
both ideas to three dimensions: the result is <term>Gauss's divergence
theorem</term>, which converts a flux integral over a closed
surface<mdash/>often a lengthy parametrized computation, as in
<xref ref="section-surface-integrals"/><mdash/>into an ordinary triple
integral.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-divergence-3d">
<title>Divergence in 3D</title>

<p>
Recall from <xref ref="eq-div-def"/> that in 2D the divergence of
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m> is
<m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = \partial M/\partial x + \partial
N/\partial y</m>, the flux density of the field at a point: positive
where the gas expands, negative where it is compressed (see
<xref ref="example-divergence-fields"/>). The three-dimensional
definition adds one term.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-divergence-3d">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = M(x,y,z)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y,z)\,\mathbf{j}
+ P(x,y,z)\,\mathbf{k}</m>. Then the divergence of <m>\mathbf{F}</m>
is defined as
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-div-3d">
\operatorname{div} \mathbf{F} = \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
= \frac{\partial M}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial N}{\partial y}
+ \frac{\partial P}{\partial z}.
</men>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
If the vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> represents the velocity field of
a flowing gas, then the divergence of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> corresponds to
the amount of compression or expansion of the gas at the point
<m>(x,y,z)</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-divergence-3d">
<title>Expanding or Compressing?</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the following velocity fields of a gas, and in each case
determine whether the gas is expanding or compressing.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F} = -x\,\mathbf{i} - y\,\mathbf{j} - z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F} = -y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf{F} = \big( x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}
\big) / \big( x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \big)^{3/2}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-divergence-3d">
<p>
We calculate the divergence of the given vector fields in each case
using <xref ref="eq-div-3d"/>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part A.</em>
<m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}
\Rightarrow \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 \gt 0</m>: the gas
is expanding, at the same rate at every point. This radial field is
shown on the left in <xref ref="fig-div3d-fields"/>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part B.</em>
<m>\mathbf{F} = -x\,\mathbf{i} - y\,\mathbf{j} - z\,\mathbf{k}
\Rightarrow \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = -3 \lt 0</m>: the gas is being
compressed; the field is the reverse of the one in part A, with all
arrows pointing toward the origin.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part C.</em>
<m>\mathbf{F} = -y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}
\Rightarrow \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 0 + 0 + 0 = 0</m>: the gas is
neither expanding nor being compressed<mdash/>this field rotates
rigidly about the <m>z</m>-axis, as on the right in
<xref ref="fig-div3d-fields"/>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part D.</em> Writing <m>\rho^2 = x^2 + y^2 + z^2</m>, a direct
computation with the quotient rule gives
</p>

<me>
\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
= \frac{3\,(x^2+y^2+z^2)^{3/2} - 3\,(x^2+y^2+z^2)^{3/2}}
{(x^2+y^2+z^2)^3} = 0.
</me>

<p>
Again, the gas is neither expanding nor being compressed: even though
the arrows point outward, they weaken with distance at exactly the
rate that preserves volume. (This is the field of a point charge in
electrostatics.) The animation in <xref ref="fig-div3d-video"/> shows
all four fields.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-div3d-video">
<caption>
Four velocity fields and their divergences: expanding
(<m>\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F} = 3</m>), compressing
(<m>\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F} = -3</m>), rotating
(<m>\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F} = 0</m>), and the inverse-square field
(<m>\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F} = 0</m>).
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-div3d"
       source="videos/divergence_3d_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-div3d-fields">
<caption>
Left: the field <m>x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
points radially outward and expands the gas
(<m>\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F} = 3</m>). Right: the field
<m>-y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}</m> rotates about the
<m>z</m>-axis without expansion (<m>\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F} = 0</m>).
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="48% 48%" valign="top">
<image xml:id="image-div3d-radial">
<shortdescription>
A 3D vector field plot with arrows radiating from the origin.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
Three-dimensional coordinate axes. Blue arrows start at the points of a
three by three by three grid around the origin and point away from the
origin in every direction, growing longer with distance: the field
<m>x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>, whose divergence
is 3.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The field F = <x, y, z> on a 3 x 3 x 3 grid of points: every arrow points away from the
// origin and grows with the distance from it, so the gas expands (div F = 3).
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(8cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={115}{18}).
triple cam = (0.8619, 0.4019, 0.3090);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(15pt)), (-1.9, 0, 0)--(2.1, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(15pt)), (0, -1.9, 0)--(0, 2.1, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(15pt)), (0, 0, -1.7)--(0, 0, 1.9), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// F at the 26 grid points other than the origin (F = 0 there), drawn at 0.38 of its length
pen fieldpen = oiblue + linewidth(1.1pt);
for (int i = -1; i <= 1; ++i)
  for (int j = -1; j <= 1; ++j)
    for (int k = -1; k <= 1; ++k) {
      triple p = (i, j, k);
      if (abs(p) > 0) draw(p--1.38*p, fieldpen, Arrow3(size=7));
    }
dot(O, linewidth(3.5pt));

label("$\mathbf{F}=x\,\mathbf{i}+y\,\mathbf{j}+z\,\mathbf{k}$", (0, 0, 2.7), oiblue + fontsize(13pt));
label("$\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F}=3$", (0, 0, -2.05), fontsize(13pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
<image xml:id="image-div3d-rotational">
<shortdescription>
A 3D vector field plot with arrows circulating around the vertical axis.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
Three-dimensional coordinate axes with three gray horizontal circles
around the <m>z</m>-axis, at heights negative one, zero and one. On each
circle eight blue arrows lie tangent to it and point counterclockwise
seen from above: the field <m>-y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}</m>, whose
divergence is 0.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The field F = <-y, x, 0> at eight points of the unit circle on three levels: the arrows
// run round the z-axis, tangent to the circles, and the gas neither expands nor compresses.
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(8cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={115}{18}).
triple cam = (0.8619, 0.4019, 0.3090);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(15pt)), (-1.9, 0, 0)--(2.1, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(15pt)), (0, -1.9, 0)--(0, 2.1, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(15pt)), (0, 0, -1.7)--(0, 0, 1.9), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the circles the gas runs round, on the levels z = -1, 0, 1
for (int k = -1; k <= 1; ++k)
  draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return (cos(t), sin(t), k); }, 0, 2pi, 72), gray(0.7) + linewidth(0.5pt));

// F at eight points of each circle, drawn at 0.45 of its length
pen fieldpen = oiblue + linewidth(1.1pt);
for (int k = -1; k <= 1; ++k)
  for (int m = 0; m < 8; ++m) {
    real a = m*pi/4;
    triple p = (cos(a), sin(a), k);
    draw(p--p + 0.45*(-sin(a), cos(a), 0), fieldpen, Arrow3(size=7));
  }

label("$\mathbf{F}=-y\,\mathbf{i}+x\,\mathbf{j}$", (0, 0, 2.7), oiblue + fontsize(13pt));
label("$\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F}=0$", (0, 0, -2.05), fontsize(13pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-gauss-theorem">
<title>Gauss's Divergence Theorem</title>

<p>
The divergence theorem relates the outward flux through the surface
boundary of a 3D region to the triple integral over the volume of the
enclosed region.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-divergence">
<title>Gauss's Divergence Theorem</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf{F}</m> be a vector field whose components have
continuous first partial derivatives, and let <m>S</m> be a piecewise
smooth oriented closed surface. The flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> across
<m>S</m> in the direction of the surface's outward unit normal field
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> equals the integral of
<m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}</m> over the region <m>D</m> enclosed by
the surface:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-divergence-theorem">
\underbrace{\iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\,
d\sigma}_{\text{outward flux through the boundary } S}
= \underbrace{\iiint_D \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\,
dV}_{\text{triple integral of the divergence over } D}.
</men>
</statement>

<proof xml:id="proof-divergence">
<p>
Write <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j} + P\,\mathbf{k}</m>.
Both sides of <xref ref="eq-divergence-theorem"/> break into three pieces
along the three components: by <xref ref="eq-div-3d"/> the right-hand side
is the sum of the integrals of
<m>\dfrac{\partial M}{\partial x}</m>, <m>\dfrac{\partial N}{\partial y}</m>
and <m>\dfrac{\partial P}{\partial z}</m>, and the left-hand side is the
sum of the fluxes of <m>M\,\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>N\,\mathbf{j}</m> and
<m>P\,\mathbf{k}</m>. So it is enough to prove the three separate
statements
</p>

<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-divthm-three" number="yes">
\iint_S M\,\mathbf{i} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
\amp = \iiint_D \frac{\partial M}{\partial x}\, dV ,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\iint_S N\,\mathbf{j} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
\amp = \iiint_D \frac{\partial N}{\partial y}\, dV ,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\iint_S P\,\mathbf{k} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
\amp = \iiint_D \frac{\partial P}{\partial z}\, dV ,
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
and add them. We prove the third; the other two are the same argument
with the axes renamed.
</p>

<p>
<em>Step 1: a solid that is simple in the <m>z</m>-direction.</em> Suppose
first that <m>D</m> can be described as in
<xref ref="sec-triple-case1"/>, that is
</p>

<me>
D = \left\{(x,y,z) : (x,y) \in R,\ u_1(x,y) \le z \le u_2(x,y)\right\} ,
</me>

<p>
where <m>R</m> is the shadow of <m>D</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane and
<m>u_1 \le u_2</m> are smooth. Its boundary <m>S</m> then falls into three
pieces, drawn in <xref ref="fig-divthm-proof-tikz"/>: the cap
<m>S_2</m> where <m>z = u_2(x,y)</m>, the floor <m>S_1</m> where
<m>z = u_1(x,y)</m>, and the vertical wall <m>S_3</m> standing over the
boundary of <m>R</m>.
</p>

<p>
On the wall the outward normal is horizontal, so
<m>\mathbf{k} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}} = 0</m> and <m>S_3</m> contributes
nothing. On the cap the outward normal points upward, and for a graph
<m>z = u_2(x,y)</m> we already know from
<xref ref="eq-flux-param"/> that
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= \left\langle -\dfrac{\partial u_2}{\partial x},\
-\dfrac{\partial u_2}{\partial y},\ 1 \right\rangle dx\, dy</m>, so that
<m>\mathbf{k} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma = dx\, dy</m>. On the floor
the outward normal points downward, which flips the sign, and
<m>\mathbf{k} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma = -dx\, dy</m>. Hence
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\iint_S P\,\mathbf{k} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
\amp = \iint_{S_2} P\,\mathbf{k} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
+ \iint_{S_1} P\,\mathbf{k} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \iint_R P\bigl(x,y,u_2(x,y)\bigr) dx\, dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad - \iint_R P\bigl(x,y,u_1(x,y)\bigr) dx\, dy .
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
<em>Step 2: the triple integral.</em> Integrating first in <m>z</m>, as in
<xref ref="eq-triple-iterated"/>, and using the Fundamental Theorem of
Calculus on the inner integral,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\iiint_D \frac{\partial P}{\partial z}\, dV
\amp = \iint_R \left[\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)}
\frac{\partial P}{\partial z}\, dz\right] dx\, dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \iint_R \Bigl[P\bigl(x,y,u_2(x,y)\bigr)
- P\bigl(x,y,u_1(x,y)\bigr)\Bigr] dx\, dy ,
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
which is exactly what Step 1 produced. So the third statement of
<xref ref="eq-divthm-three"/> holds for such a solid. Running the same
argument with <m>D</m> viewed as simple in the <m>x</m>-direction gives
the first statement, and in the <m>y</m>-direction the second. Adding the
three proves the theorem for any solid that is simple in all three
directions.
</p>

<p>
<em>Step 3: general solids.</em> A solid that is not simple in all three
directions is cut into finitely many pieces that are, exactly as a region
of the plane was cut in <xref ref="sec-general-regions-case1"/>. Apply the
theorem to each piece and add. Every cut surface is shared by two
neighbouring pieces, and it carries opposite outward normals as seen from
the two sides, so the two flux contributions differ only in sign and
cancel. What survives on the left is the flux through the original
boundary <m>S</m>, and the volume integrals simply add up to the integral
over <m>D</m>. This is the same bookkeeping that made the loop property of
<xref ref="section-conservative-fields"/> work, one dimension up.
</p>
</proof>
</theorem>

<figure xml:id="fig-divthm-proof-tikz">
<caption>
The solid of Step 1 of the proof of <xref ref="thm-divergence"/>: a solid
<m>D</m> that is simple in the <m>z</m>-direction. Its boundary is the cap
<m>S_2</m> where <m>z = u_2(x,y)</m>, the floor <m>S_1</m> where
<m>z = u_1(x,y)</m>, and the vertical wall <m>S_3</m> over the boundary of
the shadow <m>R</m>. Each piece carries its outward unit normal
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>: upward on the cap, downward on the floor, and
horizontal on the wall. On the wall <m>\mathbf{k}</m> (purple) is tangent
and meets <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> at a right angle, so
<m>\mathbf{k}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}} = 0</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-divthm-proof" width="65%">
<shortdescription>
A solid between two curved surfaces over a region in the horizontal
plane, with outward normals drawn on the top, the bottom and the side.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A solid labelled D sits above a shaded region R in the horizontal plane,
with four dashed vertical lines joining the edge of R to the solid. Its
floor, labelled S 1, is a gently curved surface and its cap, labelled S 2,
is a higher curved surface; a vertical wall, labelled S 3, joins them
around the edge. A green arrow labelled n hat points upward and outward
from the cap, another points downward and outward from the floor, and a
third points horizontally outward from the wall. At the foot of that
third arrow a purple arrow labelled k points straight up along the wall,
and a small square marks the right angle between the two.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// A solid D that is simple in the z-direction: the floor S1 (z = u1), the cap S2 (z = u2) and
// the vertical wall S3 over the boundary of the shadow R, each with its outward unit normal.
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(11.5cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={120}{20}).
triple cam = (0.8138, 0.4698, 0.3420);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (-1.7, 0, 0)--(1.9, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, -1.7, 0)--(0, 1.9, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, 0)--(0, 0, 3.1), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

real u1(real x, real y) { return 0.55 + 0.12*(x^2 - y^2); }          // the floor
real u2(real x, real y) { return 2.05 - 0.3*x*sqrt(x^2 + y^2); }      // the cap

// the shadow R in the xy-plane
triple shadow(pair q) { return (q.x*cos(q.y), q.x*sin(q.y), 0); }
draw(surface(shadow, (0.01, 0), (1, 2pi), 2, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(gray(0.8) + opacity(0.6), emissivepen=gray(0.25)));
draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return (cos(t), sin(t), 0); }, 0, 2pi, 72), gray(0.5) + linewidth(0.6pt));
// four verticals from the edge of R up to the solid
pen guide = gray(0.5) + linewidth(0.6pt) + linetype("4 3");
for (int k = 0; k < 4; ++k) {
  real a = pi/4 + k*pi/2;
  draw((cos(a), sin(a), 0)--(cos(a), sin(a), u1(cos(a), sin(a))), guide);
}

// the floor S1 and the cap S2, on a polar grid with a pinhole at the centre
triple floorS(pair q) { real x = q.x*cos(q.y), y = q.x*sin(q.y); return (x, y, u1(x, y)); }
triple capS(pair q)   { real x = q.x*cos(q.y), y = q.x*sin(q.y); return (x, y, u2(x, y)); }
draw(surface(floorS, (0.01, 0), (1, 2pi), 4, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.45*oiblue + 0.55*white + opacity(0.6),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
// the wall S3 over the unit circle, from the floor up to the cap
triple wall(pair q) {
  real x = cos(q.x), y = sin(q.x);
  return (x, y, u1(x, y) + q.y*(u2(x, y) - u1(x, y)));
}
draw(surface(wall, (0, 0), (2pi, 1), 24, 1, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.3*oiblue + 0.7*white + opacity(0.35),
                         emissivepen=0.25*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(surface(capS, (0.01, 0), (1, 2pi), 4, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.5*oiblue + 0.5*white + opacity(0.6),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
pen rim = 0.7*oiblue + 0.3*white + linewidth(0.8pt);
draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return wall((t, 0)); }, 0, 2pi, 90), rim);
draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return wall((t, 1)); }, 0, 2pi, 90), rim);

// the outward unit normals: up from the cap, down from the floor, level from the wall
pen normalpen = oigreen + linewidth(1.6pt);
real px = -0.22, py = 0.42;                       // a point seen in profile, off the z-axis
real e = 1e-4;
triple pc = (px, py, u2(px, py));
triple nc = unit((-(u2(px + e, py) - u2(px - e, py))/(2e), -(u2(px, py + e) - u2(px, py - e))/(2e), 1));
draw(pc--pc + 0.62*nc, normalpen, Arrow3(size=10));
dot(pc, oigreen + linewidth(4pt));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}$", pc + 0.62*nc, E, oigreen + fontsize(18pt));
triple pf = (px, py, u1(px, py));
triple nf = unit((0.24*px, -0.24*py, -1));        // minus the upward normal <-u1_x, -u1_y, 1>
draw(pf--pf + 0.42*nf, normalpen, Arrow3(size=10));
dot(pf, oigreen + linewidth(4pt));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}$", pf + 0.42*nf, E, oigreen + fontsize(18pt));
real aw = -0.95;                                  // on the near side of the wall
triple pw = (cos(aw), sin(aw), 1.25);
draw(pw--pw + 0.62*(cos(aw), sin(aw), 0), normalpen, Arrow3(size=10));
dot(pw, oigreen + linewidth(4pt));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}$", pw + 0.62*(cos(aw), sin(aw), 0), S, oigreen + fontsize(18pt));
// k at the same point of the wall: tangent to the wall, at a right angle to n-hat
// (drawn a hair outside the wall, so that the wall does not hide it)
triple pk = pw + 0.04*(cos(aw), sin(aw), 0);
draw(pk--pk + 0.6*Z, 0.85*oipurple + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow3(size=10));
label("$\mathbf{k}$", pk + 0.6*Z, W, 0.85*oipurple + fontsize(16pt));
real m = 0.11;
draw((pw + m*(cos(aw), sin(aw), 0))--(pw + m*(cos(aw), sin(aw), 0) + m*Z)--(pw + m*Z), gray(0.2) + linewidth(0.6pt));

// names
label("$S_2$", capS((0.75, 4.4)) + toviewer, oiblue + fontsize(18pt));
label("$S_3$", wall((0.95, 0.72)) + 2*toviewer, oiblue + fontsize(18pt));
label("$S_1$", wall((4.4, 0)), 2*W + S, oiblue + fontsize(18pt));
label("$D$", (0.25, -0.2, 1.3), 0.6*oiblue + fontsize(18pt));
label("$R$", (0.55, 0.25, 0) + toviewer, gray(0.2) + fontsize(18pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-divthm-proof-video">
<caption>
The idea of the proof, without the algebra: divergence is flux per unit
volume, so chop <m>D</m> into little boxes and add the flux out of each
one. Every interior face is shared by two boxes and carries opposite
outward normals, so the inside cancels in pairs and only the flux through
<m>S</m> is left.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-divthm-proof-idea"
       source="videos/divergence_proof_idea_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>



<example xml:id="example-divthm-sphere">
<title>Flux Through a Sphere, the Easy Way</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use the divergence theorem to compute the flux of the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
through the sphere <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-divthm-sphere">
<p>
We calculate the right-hand side of the divergence theorem,
<xref ref="eq-divergence-theorem"/>. Since
<m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 3</m>,
</p>

<me>
\iiint_D \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\, dV
= 3 \iiint_D dV
= 3\, (\text{volume of the sphere})
= 3 \left( \frac{4}{3}\pi \right) = 4\pi.
</me>

<p>
Therefore,
</p>

<me>
\iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= \iiint_D \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\, dV = 4\pi,
</me>

<p>
with no parametrization, no cross product, and no double integral in
sight: the field and its outward-pointing flux are shown in
<xref ref="fig-divthm-sphere"/>.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-divthm-sphere">
<caption>
The radial field <m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} +
z\,\mathbf{k}</m> crossing the unit sphere. By the divergence theorem
the outward flux is <m>3 \times \text{volume} = 4\pi</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-divthm-sphere" width="65%">
<shortdescription>
A sphere with arrows radiating outward through its surface.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent gray unit sphere centered at the origin. Twenty-six
vermillion arrows pierce the surface from inside, pointing radially
outward in every direction, and a dot marks the point where each one
crosses the sphere.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The radial field F = <x, y, z> crossing the unit sphere: 26 arrows, each from 0.75 to 1.35 of
// the way out along a radius, so that every one pierces the sphere.
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(10.5cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={115}{18}).
triple cam = (0.8619, 0.4019, 0.3090);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (-1.7, 0, 0)--(1.9, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, -1.7, 0)--(0, 1.9, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, -1.6)--(0, 0, 1.8), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the unit sphere, translucent
triple sph(pair q) { return (sin(q.x)*cos(q.y), sin(q.x)*sin(q.y), cos(q.x)); }
draw(surface(sph, (0, 0), (pi, 2pi), 12, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(gray(0.8) + opacity(0.35), emissivepen=gray(0.3), specularpen=gray(0.05)));
pen mesh = gray(0.6) + linewidth(0.4pt);
draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return sph((pi/2, t)); }, 0, 2pi, 72), mesh);
draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return sph((t, 0)); }, 0, 2pi, 72), mesh);
draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return sph((t, pi/2)); }, 0, 2pi, 72), mesh);

// the field along 26 radii: eight longitudes on three latitudes, and the two poles
pen fieldpen = oivermillion + linewidth(1.2pt);
void radial(triple u) {
  draw(0.75*u--1.35*u, fieldpen, Arrow3(size=10));
  dot(u, oivermillion + linewidth(3pt));           // where the arrow crosses the sphere
}
for (int m = 0; m < 8; ++m)
  for (int k = 1; k <= 3; ++k) radial(sph((k*pi/4, m*pi/4)));
radial(Z); radial(-Z);

label("$\mathbf{F}=x\,\mathbf{i}+y\,\mathbf{j}+z\,\mathbf{k}$", (0, 0, -1.95), oivermillion + fontsize(14pt));
label("$x^2+y^2+z^2=1$", 1.45*sph((0.8, -1.75)), W, gray(0.3) + fontsize(14pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-divthm-sphere-zyx">
<title>Revisiting the Flux of <m>\langle z, y, x \rangle</m></title>
<statement>
<p>
In <xref ref="example-sphere-flux"/> we found the flux of
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = z\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} +
x\,\mathbf{k}</m> across the unit sphere by parametrizing the surface,
computing <m>\mathbf{r}_\phi \times \mathbf{r}_\theta</m>, and
integrating<mdash/>a full page of work that produced
<m>\tfrac{4\pi}{3}</m>. Recompute this flux using the divergence
theorem.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-divthm-sphere-zyx">
<p>
The divergence is
</p>

<me>
\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
= \frac{\partial z}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial y}{\partial y}
+ \frac{\partial x}{\partial z} = 0 + 1 + 0 = 1,
</me>

<p>
so by <xref ref="eq-divergence-theorem"/>, with <m>S</m> the unit
sphere and <m>D</m> the unit ball <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \le 1</m>,
</p>

<me>
\iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= \iiint_D \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\, dV
= \iiint_D dV
= \text{volume of the sphere}
= \frac{4\pi}{3}(1)^3 = \frac{4\pi}{3},
</me>

<p>
in agreement with the long computation<mdash/>in one line. Watch the
two sphere computations side by side in
<xref ref="fig-divthm-sphere-video"/>.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-divthm-sphere-video">
<caption>
The divergence theorem converts the flux integrals of
<m>x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m> and
<m>z\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + x\,\mathbf{k}</m> through the unit
sphere into triple integrals: <m>3 \cdot \tfrac{4\pi}{3} = 4\pi</m>
and <m>1 \cdot \tfrac{4\pi}{3} = \tfrac{4\pi}{3}</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-divthm-sphere"
       source="videos/divthm_sphere_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-divthm-paraboloid">
<title>Revisiting the Paraboloid, the Easy Way</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate the surface integral
<m>\displaystyle \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\,
d\sigma</m>, where
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j} +
z\,\mathbf{k}</m> and the surface <m>S</m> is the boundary of the
solid region <m>E</m> enclosed by the paraboloid
<m>z = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> and the plane <m>z = 0</m>, this time using
the divergence theorem.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-divthm-paraboloid">
<p>
In <xref ref="example-paraboloid-flux"/> we computed this flux by
splitting <m>S</m> into the cap <m>S_1</m> and the disk <m>S_2</m>,
finding a normal vector for each piece, and evaluating two surface
integrals. With the divergence theorem the whole computation collapses.
The divergence is
</p>

<me>
\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
= \frac{\partial y}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial x}{\partial y}
+ \frac{\partial z}{\partial z} = 0 + 0 + 1 = 1,
</me>

<p>
so by <xref ref="eq-divergence-theorem"/>, using cylindrical
coordinates for the solid <m>E</m>
(<m>0 \le z \le 1 - r^2</m>, <m>0 \le r \le 1</m>),
</p>

<md>
<mrow>\iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
\amp= \iiint_E \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\, dV
= \iiint_E dV
= \int_0^{2\pi}\!\! \int_0^1\!\! \int_0^{1-r^2} r\, dz\, dr\,
d\theta</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 2\pi \int_0^1 (1 - r^2)\, r\, dr
= 2\pi \left( \frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{4} \right)
= \frac{\pi}{2},</mrow>
</md>

<p>
which agrees with the two-piece surface computation. Whenever the
surface is closed and the divergence is simple, the divergence theorem
is the tool of choice; see <xref ref="fig-divthm-paraboloid-video"/>.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-divthm-paraboloid-video">
<caption>
The flux of <m>y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
through the closed paraboloid-and-disk surface: the divergence theorem
replaces two surface integrals with one triple integral whose value is
<m>\pi/2</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-divthm-paraboloid"
       source="videos/divthm_paraboloid_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

</section>
